1. What is a Protocol?

A Protocol is a set of rules that tells two computers how to communicate.

Real-Life Example

Imagine two people talking on a phone.

They follow some basic rules:

One person speaks.
The other person listens.
They use a common language.
They end the call by saying "Bye."

Without rules, communication would fail.

Similarly, computers also follow rules called Protocols.

2. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
What is TCP?

TCP is a reliable protocol.

It ensures that all data reaches the destination correctly.

If any data is lost, TCP sends it again.

Features
Reliable
Connection-Oriented
Error Checking
Data is retransmitted if lost
Slower than UDP

==> How TCP Works

Before sending data,

TCP performs a 3-Way Handshake.

Step 1

Client

Can I connect?
(SYN)

↓

Step 2

Server

Yes.
(SYN + ACK)

Step 3

Client

Okay.
(ACK)

Now communication starts.

3. UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
What is UDP?

UDP is a fast protocol.

It does not check whether packets are received.

No retransmission.

Features
Fast
Connectionless
No error checking
No guarantee of delivery

4. ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol)
What is ICMP?

ICMP is used for

Error messages
Network diagnostics
Connectivity testing

ICMP does not transfer website data or files.

==> Real-Life Example

Suppose a road is closed.

The traffic police inform drivers.

ICMP also sends error messages in a network.

==> Linux Example
ping google.com

Output

64 bytes from google.com

This means the destination is reachable.

Another Example
ping 8.8.8.8

If replies come,

Internet connectivity is working.

5. DNS (Domain Name System)
What is DNS?

DNS converts a domain name into an IP address.

Humans remember names.

Computers use IP addresses.

Example

You type

www.github.com

DNS finds its IP address.

Then your browser connects to that IP.

==> Real-Life Example

Think of your phone contacts.

You search

John

Your phone finds

9876543210

DNS works the same way.

google.com

↓

142.250.xxx.xxx

==> Default Port
53

==> Linux Commands
nslookup google.com

or

dig google.com

6. DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)
What is DHCP?

DHCP automatically assigns

IP Address
Gateway
DNS Server
Subnet Mask

to devices.

Without DHCP,

you would need to configure every computer manually.

==> Real-Life Example

You visit a hotel.

The receptionist gives you a room automatically.

You don't choose it yourself.

DHCP automatically assigns an IP address in the same way.

==> Example

You connect your laptop to Wi-Fi.

Within a few seconds,

it receives

192.168.1.20

automatically.

That is DHCP.

Default Ports

Server

67

Client

68

7. HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)
What is HTTP?

HTTP is used to transfer web pages.

It is not encrypted.

Example
http://example.com

Anyone on the network could potentially read the traffic.

Port
80

8.HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure)
What is HTTPS?

HTTPS is the secure version of HTTP.

It encrypts communication using SSL/TLS.

Example
https://github.com

Your password is encrypted before it travels over the network.

==> Real-Life Example

HTTP

Sending a postcard.

Anyone can read it.

HTTPS

Sending a sealed envelope.

Only the receiver can read it.

port: 443

9. SSH (Secure Shell)
What is SSH?

SSH allows you to log in to a remote Linux server securely.

It encrypts all communication.

==> Real-Life Example

You are sitting at home.

Your AWS EC2 server is running in Mumbai.

Using SSH,

you can control the server without physically visiting the data center.

port: 22

10. FTP (File Transfer Protocol)
What is FTP?

FTP is used to transfer files between computers.

Example

Upload

backup.tar.gz

to a remote server.

or

Download

logs.zip

from the server.

==> Ports
20
21

Linux Example
ftp 192.168.1.50

11. SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)
What is SMTP?

SMTP is used for sending emails.

Example

You send an email from Gmail.

SMTP sends the email to the destination mail server.

Port
25

12. POP3 (Post Office Protocol v3)
What is POP3?

POP3 downloads emails from the mail server to your device.

By default, it may remove them from the server after downloading.

port: 110

12. IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)
What is IMAP?

IMAP allows you to read emails while keeping them on the mail server.

Changes made on one device are visible on other devices.

Real-Life Example

You check Gmail on:

Laptop
Mobile
Tablet

All devices show the same emails because IMAP keeps them synchronized.

port: 143

Note:-

What happens when you open https://github.com in your browser?", you can explain the flow:

You enter https://github.com.
DNS converts the domain name to an IP address.
TCP establishes a connection using the 3-way handshake.
HTTPS encrypts the communication using SSL/TLS.
The browser sends the HTTP request.
The server responds with the webpage.
The browser displays the GitHub page.
